    <!-- ============================================================
         NO BOOKS IN ANY OTHER BAG (v1.5.8)

         Fril, looking at a backpack full of Wiring 101 and Southern Farming: books belong in
         ONE bag, not scattered through every corpse.

         WHERE THEY WERE ACTUALLY COMING FROM, because it was not our five bags. All three of
         vanilla's zombie packs carry the whole world's book pool:

             groupZpackReg, groupZpackStrong, groupZpackBoss
                 <item group="booksAllScaled" count="1" buffs="buffPerkBookwormSuccess">

         The Ostrich's sleepers are deliberately still on vanilla loot, so every sleeper bag was a
         book bag. Our own five lists contain no book entry at all and never did, which is why
         "strip the books out of the bags" first read as already done. It was the wrong bags.

         These three removes take books out of every zombie pack in the game. The book pool itself
         is untouched, so world containers, shelves and bookcases still hold magazines exactly as
         vanilla intends. Only the corpse route changes.
         ============================================================ -->
    <remove xpath="//lootgroup[@name='groupZpackReg']/item[@group='booksAllScaled']"/>
    <remove xpath="//lootgroup[@name='groupZpackStrong']/item[@group='booksAllScaled']"/>
    <remove xpath="//lootgroup[@name='groupZpackBoss']/item[@group='booksAllScaled']"/>

    <!-- ============================================================
         THE ESSENTIAL MAGAZINES (v1.14.0)

         Fril, after a two-day run: "there were nowhere near enough books. I barely got a forge
         ahead and I need 10 for a workstation."

         WHAT IS ACTUALLY WRONG, AND IT IS NOT SUPPLY. Every block name in hotel_02_blocks.nim was
         matched against vanilla's tables: FORTY-ONE container types in the hotel can already roll
         skillMagazines. Bookshelves, book piles and file cabinets at "high", the T5 hardened
         chest at "med", then luggage, desks, lockers, nightstands and junk below that, plus the
         zombie packs at "medHigh".

         THE PROBLEM IS DILUTION. skillMagazines holds 23 entries and each reference draws one,
         so workstationSkillMagazine is about 1 in 23 a draw. Ten copies is on the order of two
         hundred magazine draws. The hotel is not short of books, it is short of THAT book.

         THE FIX IS FRIL'S OWN, FROM AREA 7. frilArea7EssentialMags is a three-entry group
         (workstation, electrical, vehicle) rolled ALONGSIDE the vanilla pool, so those draws are
         1 in 3 instead of 1 in 23. This is that pattern, and the three magazines are the same
         three, because they are the three that gate building rather than fighting.

         WHY force_prob="true" AND NOT A BARE APPEND, WHICH IS THE WHOLE CARE IN THIS CHANGE.
         The Area 7 notes record a real fault in exactly this shape: the four collapsed-workstation
         containers there got a second entry appended with no prob, and because those containers
         pick ONE entry, the magazine REPLACES the normal loot instead of accompanying it.

         Every group below is count="all", so each child fires on its own. Read out of
         LootContainer.SpawnLootItemsFromList: an entry with force_prob="true" is taken out of the
         weighted pick entirely and given its own pass/fail roll against its prob, which is what
         vanilla's own groupCrackabookShelves02 and groupLuggage02 already do one line away. So
         these entries ADD a chance of an essential magazine and take nothing away from anything
         already in the container. Nothing existing changes weight.

         prob IS A 0-TO-1 CHANCE HERE, not a weight. getProbability returns lootItem.prob
         unmodified for a group reference with no tags and no template, and forceProb then tests
         RandomFloat against it.

         THE NUMBERS, and they are choices rather than findings:
             bookshelves / book piles / single books   0.5   dedicated book furniture
             file cabinets                             0.3   an office drawer, not a library
             our own book bag                          1.0   it is THE book bag, so guarantee one

         WHAT THIS DOES NOT DO. It does not add magazine VOLUME across the hotel, only targeting
         plus these extra rolls. The volume lever is the Magazines Abundance sandbox option
         (Resources tab, option 98), which this save leaves at the vanilla 100%; Area 7 runs it at
         150%. Preset triplets if that is wanted later: DUJ = 150%, DUK = 200%, DUL = 300%. The
         other lever is Magazine Points (Crafting tab, option 108, triplet EEC for 2), which
         halves every requirement: MinEventActionAddProgressionLevel reads GameStats 115 and uses
         it in place of level="1" for a type-6 crafting progression, so one Forge Ahead would
         count as two. Neither is touched here; both are Fril's to set in the presets.

         A CORRECTION TO THE COMMENT ABOVE THIS ONE. The v1.5.8 note says removing booksAllScaled
         from the zombie packs took "the whole world's book pool" off corpses. It did not take
         MAGAZINES: booksAllScaled is perkBooks plus two schematics groups and contains no
         crafting magazines at all, and groupZpackReg02 / Strong02 / Boss02 still reference
         skillMagazines at medHigh and are untouched. That change cost perk books and schematics,
         which was the intent, and never cost a single Forge Ahead.

         NOT MEASURED: unplayed. Every structural claim here is read from vanilla's own tables and
         the assembly, but no container has been opened with this in place.
         ============================================================ -->
    <!-- TWO THINGS ABOUT THIS ONE DEFINITION, BOTH OF WHICH BIT BEFORE IT SHIPPED.

         1. The root is /lootcontainers and there is NO <lootgroups> element. My first draft used
            /lootcontainers/lootgroups and would have matched nothing, which is the exact mistake
            the v1.5.5 note two hundred lines above this one already records.

         2. A GROUP MUST BE DEFINED BEFORE ANYTHING REFERENCES IT. The second draft appended this
            to the end of /lootcontainers, and simload reported three forward references: the
            vanilla book groups sit near the top of the file and would have pointed at a group
            defined a thousand lines later. Vanilla itself never forward-references, which is why
            the check is trustworthy. So this is an insertBefore against the FIRST vanilla group
            ("empty"), which puts it ahead of every possible user. -->
    <insertBefore xpath="/lootcontainers/lootgroup[@name='empty']">
        <!-- v1.96.0: FOOD, WORKSTATION, MEDICAL. Fril: "maybe just have the foodSkillMagazine,
             workstationSkillsMagazine and the medicalSkillMagazine in it".

             Out go electricianSkillMagazine and vehiclesSkillMagazine. Vehicles because nothing
             in the hotel can be crafted that needs it and the magazine is now stripped from loot
             altogether, electrician because it was never on his list.

             Item names are vanilla's exact spellings, checked against items.xml rather than
             copied from the request: it is workstationSkillMagazine, not Skills. -->
        <lootgroup name="occEssentialMags" count="1">
            <item name="foodSkillMagazine"/>
            <item name="workstationSkillMagazine"/>
            <item name="medicalSkillMagazine"/>
        </lootgroup>
    </insertBefore>

    <append xpath="//lootgroup[@name='groupCrackabookShelves']">
        <item group="occEssentialMags" count="1" prob="0.5" force_prob="true"/>
    </append>
    <append xpath="//lootgroup[@name='groupBookPile']">
        <item group="occEssentialMags" count="1" prob="0.5" force_prob="true"/>
    </append>
    <append xpath="//lootgroup[@name='groupSingleBooks']">
        <item group="occEssentialMags" count="1" prob="0.5" force_prob="true"/>
    </append>
    <append xpath="//lootgroup[@name='groupFileCabinet']">
        <item group="occEssentialMags" count="1" prob="0.3" force_prob="true"/>
    </append>

    <!-- v1.18.0: groupBookcase, the fifth and last one.

         MEASURED PROPERLY THIS TIME, AND IT CORRECTED ME. For two sessions I told Fril the four
         appends above were aimed at groups that are not in The Ostrich and offered to retarget
         them. That was wrong, and it was wrong because I read BLOCK NAMES off the prefab and
         compared them to LOOTGROUP names. Those are two different layers. A container block
         carries LootList="crackabookShelves", which names a LOOTCONTAINER, and the lootcontainer
         is what holds <item group="groupCrackabookShelves"/>.

         Walking the real chain - the 865 block names in Ostrich_Hotel_2.blocks.nim, each resolved
         through blocks.xml (following Extends, and with LootList nested inside its class wrapper
         rather than a direct child) to a lootcontainer, then every lootcontainer expanded
         recursively through loot.xml - gives 81 containers, all 81 resolving, and 426 distinct
         lootgroups reachable. All four existing targets are among them:

             groupCrackabookShelves   via crackabookShelves   4 block types
             groupBookPile            via bookPile            2
             groupSingleBooks         via singleBooks         1
             groupFileCabinet         via fileCabinet         3

         So nothing needed retargeting. The ONE genuine gap that survey found is this: the
         "bookcase" container is in the building, on cntBookcaseCornerFull and
         cntBookcaseCornerPoorRandomLootHelper, and groupBookcase was never targeted.

         0.5 matches the other three book groups. A file cabinet is an office drawer rather than
         somewhere books live, which is why that one alone sits at 0.3. -->
    <append xpath="//lootgroup[@name='groupBookcase']">
        <item group="occEssentialMags" count="1" prob="0.5" force_prob="true"/>
    </append>

